.Dd August 20, 2026
.Dt IOPORT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ioport
.Nd create an asynchronous I/O completion port
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/ioport.h
.Ft int
.Fn ioport "unsigned nworkers"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn ioport
system call creates an asynchronous I/O completion port and returns a file
descriptor for it, referred to throughout this manual page as a
.Dq pfd .
The pfd is used with
.Xr ioevent 2
to submit asynchronous requests and to reap their completions.
.Pp
Each port created with a non-zero
.Fa nworkers
owns an independent task queue and exactly
.Fa nworkers
kernel worker threads (LWKTs).
The workers execute requests whose back end is a synchronous kernel API and
run deferred continuations of asynchronous requests.
.Pp
If
.Fa nworkers
is zero, the port is created as a
.Dq pure asynchronous
port: no worker task queue is started, and only requests with a native
asynchronous path (vnode reads and writes), together with
.Dv IO_KEVENT
and
.Dv IO_CANCEL ,
are serviced.
Any request that requires a worker completes with an
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
error in its completion record rather than silently falling back to
synchronous execution.
.Pp
The returned descriptor is an ordinary file descriptor
.Pq Dv DTYPE_IOPORT .
It can be registered with
.Xr kqueue 2 :
.Dv EVFILT_READ
on a pfd becomes ready when its completion queue is non-empty, and
.Va kevent.data
reports the number of completions currently available to be reaped.
kqueue only notifies; it does not consume completions.
Completions are reaped by
.Xr ioevent 2 .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Upon successful completion, a new pfd is returned.
Otherwise, -1 is returned and
.Va errno
is set to indicate the error.
.Sh ERRORS
The
.Fn ioport
system call will fail if:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
Insufficient memory to create the port or to start its task queue.
.It Bq Er EMFILE
The process has already reached its limit for open file descriptors.
.It Bq Er ENFILE
The system file table is full.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ioevent 2 ,
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr liburing 3
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn ioport
system call first appeared in
.Dx 6.5 .
